-- title:
--   Field-coefficient residual realizations at every level M
-- statement:
--   Let $P$ be a Frey package, so $P$ records nonzero integers $a,b,c$ with $a^p+b^p=c^p$ for a prime $p\ge 5$, together with $\gcd(a,b)=1$, $a\equiv 3 \pmod 4$, $b\equiv 0\pmod 2$, and $P$ determines the Frey curve `P.freyCurve` over $\mathbb{Q}$. The theorem asserts: for every natural number $M$ with $0<M$, equipping the degree-zero divisor class group `JZero M` of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $M$ with the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleBar M`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeModule.html#L82) over the polynomial Hecke algebra `HeckeAlg` $=\mathbb{Z}[T_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ (the structure in which `heckeGen` $\ell$ acts by the divisorial Hecke correspondence on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, when these correspondences commute), the project's predicate `P.EigenformRealizationSupplyFieldAt M` holds, which by definition means `ESMasterAttachmentSupplyField P M (JZero M)`. Unfolded, the latter says: for every $g \in S_2(\Gamma_0(M))$ satisfying the project's normalized-eigenform conditions ($a_1(g)=1$, multiplicativity of $q$-coefficients at coprime indices, and the two prime-power recursions according as the prime divides $M$ or not), and for every maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$ of the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{C}$ with $p \in \mathfrak{m}$, there exist a field $k$, a ring homomorphism $\varphi : \mathrm{HeckeAlg} \to k$, a $k$-vector space $V$, a function $\pi : \mathrm{JZero}\,M \to V$, a homomorphism $\rho$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to the $k$-linear automorphisms of $V$, a ring homomorphism $\psi$ from the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{C}$ to $k$, and a number field $F$, Galois over $\mathbb{Q}$ and embedded in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatibly with $\mathbb{Q}$, such that: $p=0$ in $k$; `IsResidualRealization` holds for these data, i.e. $V$ is finite over $k$ of rank $2$, $\pi(0)=0$, $\pi$ is additive on $p$-torsion, $\pi(\sigma\cdot x)=\rho(\sigma)\pi(x)$ and $\pi(t\cdot x)=\varphi(t)\pi(x)$ for $p$-torsion $x$, and $\pi$ of the $p$-torsion spans $V$; `CyclotomicDeterminant M p` holds for $\rho$, i.e. $\det \rho(\sigma)=\ell$ in $k$ for every prime $\ell \nmid Mp$, every valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $\ell$ and every Frobenius $\sigma$ at $\ell$ for it; $\mathfrak{m} \le \ker\psi$; for every prime $\ell \nmid Mp$ there is an algebraic integer $a$ with $a = a_\ell(g)$ in $\mathbb{C}$ and $\varphi(\mathrm{heckeGen}\,\ell)=\psi(a)$; and the kernel of restriction $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}) \to \mathrm{Gal}(F/\mathbb{Q})$ lies in the intersection of $\ker\rho$ with the kernel of the mod-$p$ Galois representation on the $p$-torsion of the Frey curve. Note that $\pi$ is only required to be additive and equivariant on $p$-torsion, and no injectivity or multiplicity-one assertion is made.
--
--   This is the Eichler–Shimura step of the Frey–Serre–Ribet route, in the form of a uniform supply of two-dimensional residual Galois representations with cyclotomic determinant attached to weight-$2$ normalized eigenforms on $\Gamma_0(M)$, realized inside the $p$-torsion of the modular Jacobian with its divisorial Hecke action. It differs from the textbook statement in two ways: the realization is packaged as a map $\pi$ from the whole Jacobian that is only additive and equivariant on $p$-torsion and whose image spans a two-dimensional space, rather than as an isomorphism onto an eigenspace; and a single number field $F$ is produced through which both $\rho$ and the Frey curve's mod-$p$ representation factor, so that the two representations can later be compared over a common finite Galois extension. The statement is vacuous at levels supporting no normalized eigenform, and is used to obtain the residual attachment family [`FreyPackage.eigenformResidualAttachmentAtFamily`](thm.html#FreyPackage.eigenformResidualAttachmentAtFamily).
